1 IF

   Runs a command, or a block of commands, only when an expression is
   true.

   Format

     IF expression THEN [$] command

     IF expression
     THEN [[$] command]
         command ...
     [ELSE [[$] command]
         command ...]
     ENDIF

2 Parameters

 expression

   A DCL expression (see Symbol_Assign Expressions).  It is true when
   its value is odd: an odd integer, a string that holds an odd number,
   or a string that starts with T or Y, in either case.

 command

   The command to run when the expression is true.

2 Description

   In the first form, the command after THEN runs when the expression
   is true.  In the second, the block form, the commands from THEN to
   ELSE (or to ENDIF, without ELSE) run when it is true, and those from
   ELSE to ENDIF when it is false.  THEN must be the command after the
   IF.

   The commands of the branch that doesn't run are passed over without
   being read, so their symbols aren't substituted.  Commands between a
   block's IF and its THEN are passed over; an ELSE among them makes
   the THEN fail (DCL-E-INVIFNEST).  Blocks may be nested, at most 15
   deep in one command level.  A GOTO out of a block ends it.

   IF, THEN, ELSE, and ENDIF leave $STATUS as it was, so a block can
   test the status of the command before it; a command after THEN or
   ELSE sets $STATUS as it would on a line of its own.

2 Examples

   1.$ IF P1 .EQS. "" THEN GOTO NO_FILE

     Goes to NO_FILE when the procedure was given no parameter.

   2.$ COPY 'P1' SAVE.DAT
     $ IF .NOT. $STATUS
     $ THEN
     $     WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "The copy failed"
     $     EXIT
     $ ENDIF

     Tests the status COPY left.
